# Copy to trainbench.env (git-ignored) and uncomment what this device needs. A variable already set in the real
# environment wins over this file. Values in force are recorded in run.json. Defaults shown are the first study
# machine's (RTX 3060); measure the new GPU's idle clock, boost peak and temperatures before changing them.

# Guards (benchkit/clockguard.py)
# TRAINBENCH_IDLE_MAX_MHZ=1000       idle SM clock must be at or below this (detects a clock lock)
# TRAINBENCH_BOOST_MHZ=1700          the boost probe must reach this SM clock
# TRAINBENCH_COOL_TO_C=60            a launch waits until the GPU is at or below this temperature ...
# TRAINBENCH_COOL_TIMEOUT_S=180      ... for at most this many seconds, then starts anyway (cooled_s records the wait)

# Browser GPU (benchkit/gpucheck.py): vendor that the WebGPU adapter and WebGL renderer must report; "any" turns it off
# TRAINBENCH_REQUIRE_GPU_VENDOR=nvidia

# Install locations (benchkit/paths.py); found automatically when the default is absent
# TRAINBENCH_TFGPU=C:\tfjsbench\tfgpu
# TRAINBENCH_CONDA=C:\ProgramData\miniconda3\Scripts\conda.exe
# TRAINBENCH_NSYS=C:\Program Files\NVIDIA Corporation\Nsight Systems 2026.5.1\target-windows-x64\nsys.exe
# TRAINBENCH_NGFX=C:\Program Files\NVIDIA Corporation\Nsight Graphics 2026.3.1
